WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.400
Most microservices talks start with how.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.000
This series starts with why

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.900
— because if we get the why wrong,

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.320
every framework we pick makes it worse.

5
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.690
Nobody actually wants microservices.

6
00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:18.910
What we want is to ship a change to one part of the system,

7
00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.010
without re-testing the whole thing.

8
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.280
That wish breaks down into five properties.

9
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:25.960
Independent deployability:

10
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.700
pricing ships without releasing checkout.

11
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:29.830
Clear ownership:

12
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:33.760
one small team owns the service, its data, and its pager.

13
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.180
Blast-radius control:

14
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.210
when inventory slows down, the cart doesn't go down with it.

15
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:40.540
Independent scaling:

16
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.310
scale the hot path, not the whole estate.

17
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.830
And technology freedom:

18
00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.800
the right tool for each job.

19
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.280
Notice that none of these say:

20
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.620
lots of processes.

21
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.360
A well-partitioned modulith

22
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.230
— one deployable, with clean service boundaries

23
00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.940
— gets you most of them.

24
00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.340
Chenile supports both.

25
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.000
And it lets you split later,

26
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.500
without rewriting your business logic.

27
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.230
The unit that matters is the service:

28
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.940
a contract, with an owner.

29
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.020
Microservices are just a deployment choice.

30
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.210
So if services are so great,

31
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.530
why does the tenth one always feel worse than the first?

32
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.570
Next episode:

33
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.780
the tax nobody budgets for.
